{
 "id": "l06",
 "label": "6",
 "title": "Pronouns and this/that",
 "summary": "I, you, he, she, we, they, and the three words for this and that",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Use the seven subject pronouns in both sentence orders",
  "Choose between tayo and kami for \"we\"",
  "Use the short form 'y after a vowel",
  "Use kayo to show respect",
  "Use ito, iyan and iyon for this and that, as subjects and as modifiers"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "The subject pronouns",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A **pronoun** stands in for a noun so you do not have to repeat it. Tagalog has seven pronouns for the subject of a sentence. They are in the **nominative** form, meaning they are used as the subject."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Start of sentence",
      "After the predicate"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "I",
       "[[ako|pron]]",
       "[[ako|pron]]"
      ],
      [
       "you (one person)",
       "[[ikaw|pron]]",
       "[[ka|pron]]"
      ],
      [
       "he or she",
       "[[siya|pron]]",
       "[[siya|pron]]"
      ],
      [
       "we (including you)",
       "[[tayo|pron]]",
       "[[tayo|pron]]"
      ],
      [
       "we (not including you)",
       "[[kami|pron]]",
       "[[kami|pron]]"
      ],
      [
       "you (plural or polite)",
       "[[kayo|pron]]",
       "[[kayo|pron]]"
      ],
      [
       "they",
       "[[sila|pron]]",
       "[[sila|pron]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Subject pronouns"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Only one pronoun changes",
     "t": "**Ikaw** is used at the start of a sentence, but it becomes **ka** when it follows the predicate. All the other pronouns stay the same in both positions."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ako ay si John.",
       "en": "I am John.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ako",
         "I",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "am",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "John",
         "John",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Masipag ako.",
       "en": "I am hardworking.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Masipag",
         "hardworking",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ako",
         "I",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ikaw ay babae.",
       "en": "You are a woman.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ikaw",
         "you",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "babae",
         "woman",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Masipag ka.",
       "en": "You are hardworking.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Masipag",
         "hardworking",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ka",
         "you",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Si Peter ay lalaki. Siya ay mabait.",
       "en": "Peter is a man. He is kind.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Peter",
         "Peter",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "lalaki",
         "man",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "Siya",
         "he",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Tap each sentence"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Two ways to say we",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "English has one \"we\". Tagalog has two, and the difference matters."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Pronoun",
      "Who is included",
      "Example situation"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[tayo|pron]]",
       "the speaker, the listener, and maybe others (you and I)",
       "Planning with friends: **Tayo** ay magkaibigan."
      ],
      [
       "[[kami|pron]]",
       "the speaker and others, but **not** the listener (we, not you)",
       "Telling a stranger about your family: **Kami** ay magkapatid."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Tayo and kami"
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Tayo ay magkaibigan.",
       "en": "We (you and I) are friends.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Tayo",
         "we, including you",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "magkaibigan",
         "friends",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Magkapatid kami.",
       "en": "We (not you) are siblings.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Magkapatid",
         "siblings",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "kami",
         "we, not including you",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Masipag tayo.",
       "en": "We (including you) are hardworking.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Masipag",
         "hardworking",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "tayo",
         "we, including you",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mahirap kami.",
       "en": "We (not you) are poor.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mahirap",
         "poor",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "kami",
         "we, not including you",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "A surprise for English speakers",
     "t": "If you tell your friend \"Let's go\", you mean you and your friend, so you use **tayo**. If you tell your friend \"We are going\" and your friend is not coming, you use **kami**. Mixing them up can sound rude or confusing."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The short form 'y",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Ay** can shrink to **'y** and attach to a word that ends in a vowel. This is very common in writing and speech."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Full form",
      "Short form",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Ako ay si John.",
       "Ako'y si John.",
       "I am John."
      ],
      [
       "Tayo ay magkaibigan.",
       "Tayo'y magkaibigan.",
       "We are friends."
      ],
      [
       "Siya ay guro.",
       "Siya'y guro.",
       "He or she is a teacher."
      ],
      [
       "Ikaw ay babae.",
       "Ikaw ay babae.",
       "You are a woman (ikaw ends in w, so ay stays)."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Full and short"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "The rule",
     "t": "Use **'y** only after a vowel. After a consonant, such as in **ikaw**, keep the full **ay**."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Showing respect: kayo and sila",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In Lesson 3 you met **po** and **kayo**. The pronoun **kayo** is the polite way to say \"you\", used for elders, strangers and authority. It is also the plural \"you\"."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Kayo po ay guro.",
       "en": "You are a teacher, sir or madam.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kayo",
         "you (respectful)",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "po",
         "polite word",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "guro",
         "teacher",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Kayo ay mababait.",
       "en": "You (plural) are kind.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kayo",
         "you (plural)",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mababait",
         "kind (plural)",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mabait po sila.",
       "en": "He or she is kind, sir or madam. (respectful)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "po",
         "polite word",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "sila",
         "they, used respectfully for one person",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Sila for one person",
     "t": "**Sila** means \"they\", but it can also be used respectfully when speaking about one older or senior person. Listen for this in conversation."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "When in doubt",
     "t": "Using **kayo** with someone you are unsure about is never wrong. You can always switch to **ka** if they invite you to."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "This and that: ito, iyan, iyon",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog has **three** words for \"this\" and \"that\". The choice depends on where the thing is, relative to you and the person you are talking to."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Word",
      "Where the thing is",
      "Meaning",
      "Sound guide"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[ito|pron]]",
       "near the speaker",
       "this",
       "i-TO"
      ],
      [
       "[[iyan|pron]]",
       "near the listener",
       "that (near you)",
       "i-YAN"
      ],
      [
       "[[iyon|pron]]",
       "far from both",
       "that (over there)",
       "i-YON"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Ito, iyan and iyon"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "As the subject"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "These words behave like pronouns. They can start a sentence with ay, or follow the predicate."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ito ay pagkain.",
       "en": "This is food.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ito",
         "this",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "pagkain",
         "food",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ito'y masarap.",
       "en": "This is delicious.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ito",
         "this",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "'y",
         "is (short for ay)",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "masarap",
         "delicious",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Maliit ito.",
       "en": "This is small.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Maliit",
         "small",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ito",
         "this",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Iyan ay aklat.",
       "en": "That (near you) is a book.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Iyan",
         "that, near you",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "aklat",
         "book",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mahaba iyon.",
       "en": "That (far away) is long.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mahaba",
         "long",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "iyon",
         "that, far away",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Ito, iyan and iyon as subjects"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "In the plural"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To say \"these\" or \"those\", put **ang mga** before them: **ang mga ito** (these), **ang mga iyan** (those near you), **ang mga iyon** (those over there)."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ang mga ito ay mabait.",
       "en": "These are kind.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "ito",
         "this",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "This and that before a noun",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Ito**, **iyan** and **iyon** can also point out a noun, like \"this table\" or \"that house\". There are two ways to build it, and both use a linker."
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Two ways to say this table",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Demonstrative first",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Itong",
         "r": "pron"
        },
        {
         "t": "mesa",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Noun first",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "mesang",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ito",
         "r": "pron"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Itong = ito + the linker -ng. In the second way, the noun carries the linker: mesa + ng."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Pattern",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Demonstrative + linker + noun",
       "[[itong mesa|noun]], [[iyang damit|noun]], [[iyong bahay|noun]]",
       "this table, that dress, that house"
      ],
      [
       "ang + noun + linker + demonstrative",
       "[[ang mesang ito|noun]], [[ang damit na iyan|noun]], [[ang bahay na iyon|noun]]",
       "this table, that dress, that house"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Pointing at a noun"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Which linker?",
     "items": [
      "**ito** becomes **itong** (the -ng linker).",
      "**iyan** becomes **iyang**, and **iyon** becomes **iyong** (the -g linker, because they end in n).",
      "After a noun, use -ng after a vowel and **na** after a consonant: mesa-ng ito, bahay na iyon."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "With sa"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When the marker **sa** (in, at, on, to) is used, the demonstrative always comes **after** the noun."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Kumain ako sa mesang ito.",
       "en": "I ate at this table.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kumain",
         "ate",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ako",
         "I",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "sa",
         "at",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "mesang",
         "table + linker",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ito",
         "this",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Pumunta kami sa bahay na iyon.",
       "en": "We went to that house.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Pumunta",
         "went",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "kami",
         "we, not including you",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "sa",
         "to",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bahay",
         "house",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "na",
         "linker",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "iyon",
         "that, far away",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "Seven subject pronouns: ako, ikaw (ka), siya, tayo, kami, kayo, sila.",
      "Tayo includes the listener. Kami leaves them out.",
      "Ay becomes 'y after a vowel.",
      "Ito = near me, iyan = near you, iyon = far from both.",
      "Itong mesa and ang mesang ito both mean this table."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
